/// \defgroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories Special power modules and their factory pairs
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Twelve of BFME 2's special-power module classes (nine
///        special-power modules and three special-ability update modules),
///        from CloudBreakSpecialPower to TaintSpecialPower, and the two
///        factory functions each one hands to the module factory: one builds
///        the module data from an INI block, the other builds the module for
///        each object.
///
/// BFME 2 registers many other special-power modules, for example
/// OCLSpecialPower, CashHackSpecialPower and DeflectSpecialPower; this group
/// covers only the twelve classes listed below.
///
/// \par From a module name to a module
/// ModuleFactory::init registers every module name together with two
/// function pointers, an interface mask and a module type. For the twelve
/// classes here the registrations (listed in
/// `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`) pair each name, spelled
/// exactly like the class, with that class's \c friend_newModuleInstance and
/// its module data class's \c friend_newModuleData. All twelve are
/// registered as logic (behavior) modules, so an `Object` block in INI
/// uses one on a `Behavior = DarknessSpecialPower ModuleTag_...` line; the
/// tag after the name is the author's choice and identifies the module
/// within its object (\ref page_modding_modules).
///
/// The two factories run at different times:
/// 1. **While INI loads.** ThingTemplate::parseModuleName hands the module
///    line to ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, which looks the name up
///    and calls the registered data factory with the INI reader. The data
///    factory allocates the module data, runs its constructor (which sets
///    every field's default) and, when the INI pointer is not null, calls
///    INI::initFromINIMultiProc with the new data and the class's static
///    \c buildFieldParse. That function chains the field tables: the base
///    class's table first, then the class's own, so the block accepts every
///    field of both. Back in ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI the
///    module tag's name key is stored in the data and the data is appended
///    to the factory's own list.
/// 2. **When an object is built.** Object::Object creates one module per
///    entry of the template's behavior list through ModuleFactory::newModule,
///    which looks the name up again and calls the registered instance
///    factory with the new object and the module data parsed in step 1.
///    The instance factory allocates the module and runs its constructor.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data exists per module line per template. It is shared,
///   read-only in practice, by every object built from that template, and
///   the module factory keeps it in its list. Zero Hour: the module
///   factory's destructor deletes that list.
/// - One module exists per object that lists the module, and lives as long
///   as the object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the 24 functions
/// - Every instance factory has the same shape: allocate with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, construct,
///   and return the pointer as the base type the module factory stores.
///   Every data factory does the same and adds the INI step above. An
///   exception frame around the constructor frees the block if the
///   constructor throws, as a C++ new-expression requires.
/// - Zero Hour generates the same pair inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`. There, the module comes from the class's own memory
///   pool and the data from a tagged \c new; BFME 2's bodies call the
///   global \c operator \c new for both.
/// - A null INI pointer leaves the data at its constructor defaults. The
///   BFME 2 bodies test for it, as Zero Hour's macro does; no BFME 2 caller
///   that passes null is identified here.
/// - Neither factory is called directly: the module factory reaches them
///   only through the stored pointers, so no direct caller is known.
/// - The interface masks in the registrations are read with Zero Hour's bit
///   values, which BFME 2's registrations fit: AutoHealBehavior's mask, for
///   example, is update, damage and upgrade, as in Zero Hour.
///
/// \par Two families behind one naming pattern
/// - **Special power modules** (nine): CloudBreakSpecialPower,
///   DarknessSpecialPower, DevastateSpecialPower, ElvenWoodSpecialPower,
///   FreezingRainSpecialPower, GrabPassengerSpecialPower,
///   InvisibilitySpecialPower, PlayerHealSpecialPower and
///   TaintSpecialPower. Their registrations carry the special-power
///   interface. Each data class's \c buildFieldParse starts from
///   SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, whose shared table includes
///   \c SpecialPowerTemplate, \c UpdateModuleStartsAttack, \c StartsPaused,
///   \c InitiateSound, \c InitiateFX, \c TriggerFX and the
///   \c AttributeModifier fields. Their constructors only run the
///   SpecialPowerModule constructor, and they are allocated at the same
///   size as OCLSpecialPower, so their per-object state is the base
///   class's (Zero Hour: recharge and pause bookkeeping). What each power
///   does lives in its versions of the three special-power entry points
///   (\c doSpecialPower with no target, \c doSpecialPowerAtObject and
///   \c doSpecialPowerAtLocation). Each class page says, for all three,
///   whether the class replaces it or keeps SpecialPowerModule's, as read
///   from the slots of each class's special-power interface table in the
///   retail executable. Some replacements are one piece of code that the
///   executable shares between several classes.
/// - **Special-ability update modules** (three): DominateEnemySpecialPower,
///   GiveOrRestoreUpgradeSpecialPower and LevelGrantSpecialPower. Despite
///   the name, their registrations carry the update interface, not the
///   special-power one. Each data class's \c buildFieldParse starts from
///   SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, whose shared table
///   includes \c SpecialPowerTemplate, \c StartAbilityRange,
///   \c PreparationTime, \c PackTime, \c UnpackTime, \c EffectDuration and
///   many more. LevelGrantSpecialPower overrides a virtual
///   \c triggerAbilityEffect (LevelGrantSpecialPower::triggerAbilityEffect),
///   and the other two override the same virtual function.
///   Open-BFME-1's reconstruction derives DominateEnemySpecialPower and
///   LevelGrantSpecialPower from SpecialAbilityUpdate; this page does not
///   name the BFME 2 base class. Zero Hour: SpecialAbilityUpdate's
///   triggerAbilityEffect fires the effect once the preparation time has
///   passed; a SpecialPowerModule's entry points offer the command to the
///   object's special-power update modules, and with
///   \c UpdateModuleStartsAttack set they leave triggering the power to
///   that update module.
///
/// \par Origins
/// None of the twelve classes is in Zero Hour. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree
/// has sources for ten of them; GiveOrRestoreUpgradeSpecialPower and
/// InvisibilitySpecialPower do not appear there. The INI field names on
/// the class pages are read from the retail executable's own field tables,
/// which the matched \c buildFieldParse functions register.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, and every
///   field listed on the class pages plus the shared base-table fields.
///   The special power itself is a separate \c SpecialPower definition
///   (\ref page_core_frameworks_upgrades), which the module names through
///   its \c SpecialPowerTemplate field.
/// - Hard-coded: the twelve module names and what each one does with its
///   fields. A new kind of power needs a new class and a new
///   ModuleFactory::init registration.
/// - Fields parsed as object or button names (for example \c TaintObject,
///   \c SunbeamObject, \c CommandButton) are stored as text when the INI is
///   read; this page does not establish when or whether a misspelled name
///   is reported.
/// - Multiplayer: all twelve run in the game logic, and their module data
///   comes from `Object` blocks, which the thing factory loads through the
///   CRC transfer (\ref sub_rts_model_modding). Every machine must load the
///   same values.
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle), so renaming a tag in
///   a mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Duration fields go through INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt. Among them are
/// \c Duration on InvisibilitySpecialPower, \c WeatherDuration in the shared
/// special-power table, and \c PreparationTime in the shared
/// special-ability table, which of the twelve classes here only the three
/// update-based modules use.
/// The parser multiplies the written milliseconds by a frames-per-millisecond
/// scale held in a global and rounds up, so the module data stores whole
/// logic frames. BFME 2's frame-rate setup recomputes that scale from the
/// logic frames per second it is given; Zero Hour uses a compile-time
/// constant. The conversion happens once, when the INI is read, so values
/// already parsed keep the frame counts of the rate in force at that time.
/// \ref page_frame_loop describes what else depends on the logic rate; any
/// change to it breaks play with unmodified clients and replays.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class CloudBreakSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Special power module registered as "CloudBreakSpecialPower": a
///        power used with a target location whose own effect covers the
///        whole map, configured by a radius, an FX list, an object name and
///        a spacing.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object state of its own
/// (see the group page). It replaces all three entry points:
/// - CloudBreakSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtLocation does nothing when its
///   object is disabled or no location is given; otherwise it runs
///   SpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPowerAtLocation and then the class's own
///   steps. Those include a map-wide step that does not use the target
///   location: it reads the map extent from the terrain logic, plays
///   \c CloudBreakFX (when set) at the centre of the map on the ground
///   and, when \c ObjectSpacing is greater than 1, creates the
///   \c SunbeamObject on the ground at every point of a grid with that
///   spacing covering the whole map inside a border one spacing wide.
///   Grid coordinates are whole numbers. When no object template has the
///   \c SunbeamObject name, no sunbeam objects are created. Further steps
///   follow it; they are not described here.
/// - The untargeted and object-targeted entry points are the same code as
///   ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPower and
///   ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtObject; the executable holds
///   one copy for this class, DarknessSpecialPower, FreezingRainSpecialPower,
///   TaintSpecialPower and ElvenWoodSpecialPower. Both do nothing while the
///   object is disabled; otherwise they call this class's location form at
///   the object's own position or at the target's position.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData, after the shared
/// special-power fields: \c CloudBreakRadius (real), \c CloudBreakFX (FX
/// list), \c SunbeamObject (an object name, stored as text) and
/// \c ObjectSpacing (real). \c SunbeamObject and \c ObjectSpacing drive
/// the map-wide grid described above: wherever the power is aimed, the
/// sunbeam objects cover the whole map and \c CloudBreakFX plays at the
/// map centre, not around the target. A spacing of 1 or less places no
/// sunbeam objects, and a smaller spacing places more of them.
///
/// \note BFME 2 also has \c CloudBreakEffect weather definitions
///       (\ref sub_gameclient); how this module relates to them is not
///       established here.
/// \see CloudBreakSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for CloudBreakSpecialPower: the shared special-power
///        fields plus the cloud-break radius, FX, sunbeam object and
///        spacing.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template. The field list is on
/// CloudBreakSpecialPower.

/// \fn Module *CloudBreakSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "CloudBreakSpecialPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "CloudBreakSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The fields come from
/// CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, which chains the
/// shared special-power table and the class's own.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see CloudBreakSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class DarknessSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Special power module registered as "DarknessSpecialPower": a
///        power used with a target location whose own FX plays at the map
///        centre, configured by a radius and an FX list.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object state of its own
/// (see the group page). It replaces all three entry points:
/// - DarknessSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtLocation does nothing when its
///   object is disabled or no location is given; otherwise it runs
///   SpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPowerAtLocation and then the class's own
///   steps. Those include a map-wide FX step: \c DarknessFX plays at the
///   centre of the map (taken from the terrain extent) at the height of
///   the target location; the target's horizontal position is not used.
///   The step is skipped when no FX is set or when the global weather
///   system's state already has one particular value (a different value
///   from FreezingRainSpecialPower's; what the values mean is not
///   established here). A further step follows it; it is not described
///   here.
/// - The untargeted and object-targeted entry points are the same code as
///   ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPower and
///   ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtObject (one copy in the
///   executable, shared with CloudBreakSpecialPower and others). Both do
///   nothing while the object is disabled; otherwise they call this class's
///   location form at the object's own position or at the target's
///   position.
///
/// Open-BFME-1 has a BFME 1 version of the class.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData, after the shared
/// special-power fields: \c DarknessRadius (real) and \c DarknessFX (FX
/// list).
/// \see DarknessSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for DarknessSpecialPower: the shared special-power
///        fields plus a radius and an FX list.
///
/// Built by DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the
/// field list is on DarknessSpecialPower.

/// \fn Module *DarknessSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DarknessSpecialPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DarknessSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
/// with DarknessSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse as the field
/// source.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see DarknessSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class DevastateSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Special power module registered as "DevastateSpecialPower": a
///        power applied at a location or at a target object's position,
///        with a radius, an FX list, tree-value settings and a weapon name.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object state of its own
/// (see the group page). It replaces all three entry points:
/// - DevastateSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtLocation does the work.
/// - The object-targeted entry point calls the location form at the
///   target's position.
/// - The untargeted entry point does not use the power; at most it issues
///   a debug report that the power needs a target object or a location.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData, after the shared
/// special-power fields: \c Radius (real), \c FX (FX list),
/// \c TreeValueMultiplier (a percentage),
/// \c TreeValueTotalCap (real) and \c FireWeapon (a weapon name, stored as
/// text). Open-BFME-1's BFME 1 table has the first four; \c FireWeapon is
/// new in BFME 2.
/// \see DevastateSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for DevastateSpecialPower: the shared special-power
///        fields plus radius, FX, tree-value multiplier and cap, and weapon.
///
/// Built by DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the
/// field list is on DevastateSpecialPower.

/// \fn Module *DevastateSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DevastateSpecialPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DevastateSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
/// with DevastateSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse as the field
/// source.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see DevastateSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class DominateEnemySpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Special-ability update module registered as
///        "DominateEnemySpecialPower", configured by a domination radius,
///        two FX lists, a permanent-conversion flag and a target filter.
///
/// Registered with the update interface, not the special-power one (see
/// the group page). It overrides the same special-ability trigger function
/// as LevelGrantSpecialPower::triggerAbilityEffect. Open-BFME-1 derives the
/// BFME 1 class from SpecialAbilityUpdate.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData, after the shared
/// special-ability fields: \c DominateRadius (real), \c TriggerFX and
/// \c DominatedFX (FX lists), \c PermanentlyConvert (bool) and
/// \c AttributeModifierAffects (an object filter). By the names, the power
/// takes control of enemies in the radius; the effect itself is in the
/// trigger code, which this page does not describe.
/// \see DominateEnemySpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for DominateEnemySpecialPower: the shared
///        special-ability fields plus radius, two FX lists, the permanence
///        flag and a target filter.
///
/// Built by DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the
/// field list is on DominateEnemySpecialPower.

/// \fn Module *DominateEnemySpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DominateEnemySpecialPower":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance; only
/// the allocation size differs.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared
///                    DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DominateEnemySpecialPower": creates
///        the module data and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
/// with DominateEnemySpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse (chained on
/// the special-ability table) as the field source.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see DominateEnemySpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class ElvenWoodSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Special power module registered as "ElvenWoodSpecialPower":
///        an area power configured by grove objects, a radius, an FX list
///        and an object creation list.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object state of its own
/// (see the group page). It replaces all three entry points, and the two
/// that take no location forward to the location form; both do nothing
/// while the object is disabled:
/// - ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPower uses its own object's position;
/// - ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtObject uses the target's
///   position;
/// - ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtLocation does nothing when its
///   object is disabled, no location is given or \c ElvenGroveObject is
///   empty; otherwise it runs SpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPowerAtLocation
///   and then the class's own work at the location.
///
/// The executable holds one copy of the first two for this class,
/// CloudBreakSpecialPower, DarknessSpecialPower, FreezingRainSpecialPower
/// and TaintSpecialPower; the call to the location form goes through the
/// virtual table, so each class reaches its own location form.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData, after the shared
/// special-power fields: \c ElvenGroveObject (an object name, stored as
/// text; the power does nothing while it is empty), \c ElvenNumObjects
/// (integer), \c ElvenWoodObject (read by a dedicated parser; its format
/// is not described here), \c ElvenWoodRadius
/// (real), \c ElvenWoodFX (FX list) and \c ElvenWoodOCL (object creation
/// list).
/// \see ElvenWoodSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for ElvenWoodSpecialPower: the shared special-power
///        fields plus grove object, counts, radius, FX and object creation
///        list.
///
/// Built by ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the
/// field list is on ElvenWoodSpecialPower.

/// \fn Module *ElvenWoodSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ElvenWoodSpecialPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as a Module pointer.
/// \see ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ElvenWoodSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as CloudBreakSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
/// with ElvenWoodSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse as the field
/// source.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null.
/// \return The new module data, as a ModuleData pointer.
/// \see ElvenWoodSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class FreezingRainSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Special power module registered as "FreezingRainSpecialPower": a
///        power used with a target location whose own FX plays at the map
///        centre, with a radius, an FX list and a burn-rate modifier.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object state of its own
/// (see the group page). It replaces all three entry points:
/// - FreezingRainSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtLocation does nothing when
///   its object is disabled or no location is given; otherwise it runs
///   SpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPowerAtLocation, then a map-wide FX step,
///   and then hands \c BurnRateModifier to a global manager whose role is
///   not identified here. In the FX step \c FreezingRainFX plays at the
///   centre of the map (taken from the terrain extent) at the height of
///   the target location; the target's horizontal position is not used.
///   The step is skipped when no FX is set or when the global weather
///   system's state already has one particular value (a different value
///   from DarknessSpecialPower's; what the values mean is not established
///   here).
/// - The untargeted and object-targeted entry points are the same code as
///   ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPower and
///   ElvenWoodSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtObject (one copy in the
///   executable, shared with CloudBreakSpecialPower and others). Both do
///   nothing while the object is disabled; otherwise they call this class's
///   location form at the object's own position or at the target's
///   position.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of FreezingRainSpecialPowerModuleData, after the shared
/// special-power fields: \c FreezingRainRadius (real), \c FreezingRainFX
/// (FX list) and \c BurnRateModifier (integer).
/// \see FreezingRainSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      FreezingRainSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class FreezingRainSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for FreezingRainSpecialPower: the shared
///        special-power fields plus radius, FX and burn-rate modifier.
///
/// Built by FreezingRainSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the
/// field list is on FreezingRainSpecialPower.

